# Template for .env. Make your own copy, then fill in the values:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Do this on your computer (for trunk serve) and on the server (for
# docker compose). .env is listed in .gitignore and .dockerignore, so it never
# reaches GitHub and is never copied into a Docker image.

# ── Shop details kept out of the code ──────────────────────────────────────
# shop.toml reads these as ${SHOP_DISCORD:-…} and ${SHOP_EMAIL:-…}, and the
# site shows them on its contact card. They aren't secret, as every visitor
# sees them, but keeping them here keeps them out of the code and GitHub.
# Left empty, the page shows shop.toml's examples instead, and the build warns
# you. Once the site is live (demo = false in shop.toml), the build stops
# instead, so visitors never see the examples. Anything named SHOP_… is
# published on the page, so never use that prefix for a real secret.
SHOP_DISCORD=
SHOP_EMAIL=

# ── Orders by email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The order panel's Send by email (send_by_email in shop.toml) goes to the
# orders service (see compose.yaml), which emails each order through Postmark
# to ORDER_EMAIL_TO, from ORDER_EMAIL_FROM. Replying to the email answers the
# buyer: its Reply-To is the address they gave. They get no copy. Both
# addresses must be on a domain or sender signature you've confirmed in
# Postmark. Until these and POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN (under Secrets) are set,
# the order panel says sending by email isn't ready yet. These aren't
# secret, but keeping them here keeps them out of the code and GitHub.
ORDER_EMAIL_FROM=
ORDER_EMAIL_TO=
# Optional. Left out, they are:
#   POSTMARK_SMTP_PORT     587; use 2525 if your server's host blocks 587
#   ORDERS_PER_HOUR        3 orders an hour from one visitor, or one address
#   ORDERS_PER_DAY         20 orders a day for the whole shop

# ── Messages from the contact page ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The contact page's form goes to the orders service too, which emails each
# message through Postmark to CONTACT_EMAIL_TO, from CONTACT_EMAIL_FROM, with
# the visitor's name beside it. Replying to the email answers the visitor:
# its Reply-To is the address they gave. They get no copy. Both addresses
# must be on a domain or sender signature you've confirmed in Postmark. Until
# these and POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN (under Secrets) are set, the contact page
# says sending messages isn't ready yet. Like the order addresses, these
# aren't secret, but keeping them here keeps them out of the code and GitHub.
CONTACT_EMAIL_FROM=
CONTACT_EMAIL_TO=
# Optional. Left out, they are:
#   MESSAGES_PER_HOUR      3 messages an hour from one visitor, or one address
#   MESSAGES_PER_DAY       20 messages a day for the whole site

# ── Secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Anything truly secret this project needs (API keys, tokens, passwords) goes
# in .env too, under a name that doesn't start with SHOP_. List its name in
# this file without the value, and pass it to the container that needs it
# under `environment:` in compose.yaml, never as a build argument.

# Postmark's server token, from your Postmark server's API Tokens tab. The
# orders service uses it as both the SMTP user name and the password, for
# orders and for messages from the contact page. Only the orders container is
# given it.
POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=
